[LINK: (Web content) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Notability_(web)]

[LINK: (Notability essays) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Essay_directory#Notability]

[LINK: (Guide to deletion) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Guide_to_deletion]

[LINK: (Common deletion outcomes) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Articles_for_deletion/Common_outcomes]

[LINK: (Why was my article deleted?) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Why_was_the_page_I_created_deleted%3F]

[LINK: (v) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Notability_sidebar]

[LINK: (t) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_talk:Notability_sidebar]

[LINK: (e) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:EditPage/Template:Notability_sidebar]

Your topic must meet Wikipedia's criteria for having an article, which we call " [LINK: (notability) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Notability] ". If there are multiple published  [LINK: (reliable sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RS]  about a topic, then it may be notable. A topic is not notable if there aren't sufficient reliable sources about it. (Important: unreliable ones like  [LINK: (blogs) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLOG] ,  [LINK: (social media) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SOCIALMEDIA] , and  [LINK: (websites anyone can edit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:UGC]  don't count!) The  [LINK: (general notability guideline) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:GNG]  gives more details on how to tell if a topic is notable or not. If you're not sure if your topic is notable, ask for help at the  [LINK: (Teahouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:TEA] .

A notable topic is one that has multiple reliable sources that each meet all three of the following criteria:

Secondary. A  [LINK: (secondary source) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Secondary]  provides thought and reflection based on primary sources, generally at least one step removed from a topic. It contains analysis, evaluation, interpretation, or synthesis of the facts, evidence, concepts, and ideas taken from  [LINK: (primary sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Primary] .

Independent of the subject. This means that nobody involved in writing or publishing the source has a financial or other connection to the subject.  A source is not  [LINK: (independent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:IS]  of the subject if any of the following are true:
if the subject paid for it, including all forms of  [LINK: (sponsored content) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sponsored_content] ,
if the content within the source originates from the subject, including most  [LINK: (interviews) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Interviews]  and all  [LINK: (press releases) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Press_releases] , even if published by a third party (sometimes called " [LINK: (churnalism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churnalism] "), or
it was  [LINK: (published by the subject itself) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SPS] , on behalf of the subject, or by someone related to the subject.

if the subject paid for it, including all forms of  [LINK: (sponsored content) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sponsored_content] ,

if the content within the source originates from the subject, including most  [LINK: (interviews) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Interviews]  and all  [LINK: (press releases) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Press_releases] , even if published by a third party (sometimes called " [LINK: (churnalism) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Churnalism] "), or

it was  [LINK: (published by the subject itself) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SPS] , on behalf of the subject, or by someone related to the subject.

Significant coverage. The sources must discuss your subject  [LINK: (in depth) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SIGCOV] , in multiple paragraphs. Trivial mentions, such as one or two sentences or the appearance of your subject in a table or list, is not enough to help establish notability. The special notability guideline for businesses has  [LINK: (lots of good examples) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CORPDEPTH]  of what is considered  [LINK: (significant) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SUBSTANTIAL]  and  [LINK: (trivial) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CORPTRIV] .

If your topic is not notable, stop here and find a new one. Non-notable topics do not qualify for a separate article. Consider expanding a relevant existing article related to the topic or select a new topic. If you are stuck, ask for guidance at the  [LINK: (Teahouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Teahouse] .

It's a good idea to look at several existing Wikipedia articles on subjects similar to yours to see how such articles are formatted. The quality of our existing articles varies and lower quality articles should not be used as a model. The  [LINK: (Talk page) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Talk_pages]  of the article may have a  [LINK: (quality rating) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:ASSESS]  in the shaded box at the top. If an article has been assessed as B-class, or as a Good Article or Featured Article, it is safe to use as an organizational template for your article. You can also consult Wikipedia's  [LINK: (Manual of Style) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MOS:ORDER]  for guidance on how to structure an article.


# WRITING YOUR DRAFT #

Once you've verified that there  [LINK: (§ isn't already an article out there) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Your_first_article#Search_for_an_existing_article]  about your topic,  [LINK: (§ gathered your sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Your_first_article#Gather_sources] , and  [LINK: (§ established notability) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Your_first_article#Notability_–_should_this_topic_have_an_article?] , you're ready to start writing!

Wikipedia provides  [LINK: (various editing tools) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Editing#Edit_screen(s)] , and you have a choice of what tool best suits your needs:

[LINK: (Source editor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Introduction_to_editing_with_Wiki_Markup/1]  – edits the  [LINK: (wikitext) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Wikitext]  of the article, which uses some special characters, like adding [[square brackets]] to create a link to another page, or asterisks to make bullet points.

[LINK: (Visual Editor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Introduction_to_editing_with_VisualEditor/1]  – a tool similar to a word processor, for editing articles without the need to understand any special codes or  [LINK: (markup) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Markup_language] .

[LINK: (Visual Editor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Visual_Editor]  is the default. To switch your default editing interface, select an option from the "Editing mode" menu in  [LINK: (your preferences) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:Preferences#mw-prefsection-editing] .

Users of  [LINK: (mobile devices) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Mobile_access]  are automatically redirected to the  [LINK: (official mobile version) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Mobile_access#Official_mobile_version]  of Wikipedia. If you don't want to use the mobile version, tap the  [LINK: (Desktop) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Mobile_access#Desktop_access]  link at the bottom of any page to switch to the desktop version.

Where will you develop your draft? You have several options:

in " [LINK: (Draftspace) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Drafts] " – this is a special area of Wikipedia dedicated to creating new articles, and is a good choice because other editors can find your draft here and help out.

in  [LINK: (your sandbox) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:About_the_sandbox]  – this is a page you can always easily find, by tapping the user icon in the top right corner to show the menu linking your sandbox. Downside: you can only create one article at a time there, and it's not so easy for other editors to find.

in a  [LINK: (user subpage) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:USERSUBPAGE] . You can find more information about subpages  [LINK: (here) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:SUBPAGE] .

The easiest way is with the Article Wizard, which will create your article in Draft space and guide you through the steps of creating a draft.

Writing a Wikipedia article:

Summarize the most important things your sources say.
Don't  [LINK: (copy/paste) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:COPYRIGHT]  wording from your sources; instead,  [LINK: (summarize the ideas in the source) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:FOLLOWSOURCE]  using your own words. Summarization is more than just  [LINK: (changing a few words around) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CLOP]  here and there.
Only add information  [LINK: (supported by your sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:V] . Don't add from  [LINK: (your own knowledge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NOR]  or expertise.

Don't  [LINK: (copy/paste) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:COPYRIGHT]  wording from your sources; instead,  [LINK: (summarize the ideas in the source) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:FOLLOWSOURCE]  using your own words. Summarization is more than just  [LINK: (changing a few words around) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CLOP]  here and there.

Only add information  [LINK: (supported by your sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:V] . Don't add from  [LINK: (your own knowledge) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NOR]  or expertise.

Make sure you  [LINK: (show all major viewpoints) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:DUE]  fairly. Do not cover  [LINK: (fringe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:FRINGE]  views or those held only by a tiny minority.

Don't  [LINK: (take sides) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NPOV]  or use  [LINK: (promotional language) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:PROMO] .

Write in a  [LINK: (professional tone) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:TONE] ; avoid  [LINK: (loaded) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MOS:PUFFERY]  language.

Add  [LINK: (citations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CITE]  as you go. This is much easier than  [LINK: (writing first and trying to remember later) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BACKWARD]  where you found each piece of information.

You don't have to write the article all at once! Save your progress frequently, with an appropriate  [LINK: (edit summary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:EDITSUMMARY] . The Publish button saves your progress.

You can  [LINK: (add images) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Pictures] ,  [LINK: (templates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Template]  like an  [LINK: (infobox) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Infobox]  at the top, or a  [LINK: (stub) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Stub]  at the bottom, and  [LINK: (categories) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Categories]  to your article.

Avoid using  [LINK: (large language models) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:LLM]  ("AI") like  [LINK: (ChatGPT) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ChatGPT]  to generate article content. These often produce inaccurate text, and sometimes make up fake sources and references. It is much better to write the article completely yourself - then you can be confident in what it says. Remember, you are responsible for the content you add.

Sources are the published books, academic articles, reputable magazines and newspapers, and other locations where you find the information you will be writing about. You will need to find sources before you start writing, because all content in articles at Wikipedia must be  [LINK: (verifiable) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:V] —that is, backed up by  [LINK: (reliable sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RS] .

The best way to demonstrate verifiability, is by creating a  [LINK: (citation) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CITE]  to a reliable source that you found, and  [LINK: (embedding it in-line) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:INLINECITE]  as you write your article.

The best sources tend to have some form of editorial oversight and a reputation for fact-checking and accuracy. Some websites are reliable; many are not. If you're not sure about the reliability of a website, you can  [LINK: (read what editors think about them) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RSP] . It's okay to use  [LINK: (non-English sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NONENG] ,  [LINK: (offline sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:OFFLINE] , and subscription-only or  [LINK: (fee-based sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:PAYWALL] , as long as they are reliable.

Here are some "Do"s and "Don't"s, to highlight some of the most important points about writing your first article:

Do make sure that the topic you write about is  [LINK: (notable) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:N] ; articles about non-notable topics get deleted.

Do include  [LINK: (citations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:CITE]  to  [LINK: (independent) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Independent_sources] ,  [LINK: (reliable) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RS]  sources for all assertions of fact.

Do add content that has a  [LINK: (neutral point of view) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NPOV] , and  [LINK: (fairly represents the majority) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:DUE_WEIGHT]  of the sources.

Do exercise caution when editing articles about  [LINK: (living people) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Biographies_of_living_persons] . Negative or critical content must be impeccably sourced to  [LINK: (highly reliable sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLPSOURCES] .

Do ask for help at the  [LINK: (Teahouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Teahouse] , the  [LINK: (Help desk) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Help_desk] , or check  [LINK: (these help links) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Directory] .

Don't engage in  [LINK: (original research) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:No_original_research]  or personal essays.

Don't copy  [LINK: (copyrighted material) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Copyrights]  into articles.

Don't speculate about  [LINK: (events in the future) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NOTCRYSTAL] .

Don't write an article only to define a word; Wikipedia is  [LINK: (not a dictionary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NOTDICT] . Try  [LINK: (Wiktionary) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wiktionary]  instead.

Don't write an article if you have a  [LINK: (conflict of interest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:COI]  because of a personal or professional connection to the topic of the article.

Don't use  [LINK: (large language models) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:LLM]  (LLMs) to write original content.


# PUBLISH YOUR DRAFT #

When your draft is ready, you have two options for publishing it: you can do so directly yourself, or ask another editor to review it first. If you don't have an account, if your account is not yet  [LINK: (confirmed) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:User_access_levels#Autoconfirmed_and_confirmed_users] , or if you have a  [LINK: (conflict of interest) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Conflict_of_interest]  with the article topic, you will have to ask for a review. Otherwise, the choice is up to you. In either case, you should take some time to double check that the draft is ready first.

Take another look at your draft to see if it meets the criteria required for a Wikipedia article. Make sure that:

The  [LINK: (first sentence) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MOS:LEADSENTENCE]  defines the article topic, and tells the reader who or what the subject is in plain English.

There are citations to  [LINK: (reliable sources) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:RS]  in the article (most reviewers look for at least three sources).
Some  [LINK: (specific types) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:MINREF]  of information require  [LINK: (inline citations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:INLINECITE] , namely:  [LINK: (direct quotations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WHYCITE] , and any  [LINK: (contentious material) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLPCT]  about  [LINK: (living persons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLP]  (negative, positive, or neutral).

Some  [LINK: (specific types) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:MINREF]  of information require  [LINK: (inline citations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:INLINECITE] , namely:  [LINK: (direct quotations) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:WHYCITE] , and any  [LINK: (contentious material) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLPCT]  about  [LINK: (living persons) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLP]  (negative, positive, or neutral).

The article is written from a  [LINK: (neutral point of view) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:NPOV] .

Any controversial claims about  [LINK: (living people) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:BLP]  are impeccably sourced.

If you are sure your draft is ready, and you do not have a  [LINK: (§ conflict of interest with the topic) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Your_first_article#Are_you_connected_to_the_article_topic?] , you can publish it directly by moving the page from the draft namespace to the main article namespace. To do so, follow the instructions in  [LINK: (how to move a page) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:How_to_move_a_page] . Articles published directly take longer to appear in external search engines, up to a maximum of  [LINK: (90 days) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Controlling_search_engine_indexing] .

Alternatively, ifIf you think your article is ready but would like another editor to check it, you can  [LINK: (submit it for review) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:AFCREVIEW]  by an experienced editor by clicking the Submit the draft for review! button at the top of your draft. If the button isn't there, you can instead add {{ [LINK: (subst:submit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Submit] }} to the top of the draft. A reviewer will then look at your draft and either publish it or give you feedback on how to improve it. You may continue to edit the page, even while waiting for a review. This may take some time (maybe weeks); please have patience, we are all volunteers.

Congratulations, you've created your first article! We have some suggested next steps at  [LINK: (Help:After your first article) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:After_your_first_article] , but do pat yourself on the back. Not many people have successfully created an article on Wikipedia!


# HELP IS AVAILABLE! #

One of the best places to ask for assistance is at the  [LINK: (Teahouse) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Teahouse] , which is dedicated to answering newer editors' questions.

Contact  [LINK: (your Wikipedia mentor) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Mentorship#How_do_I_ask_my_mentor_a_question?]  – they are there to answer your questions!

For a list of informative, instructional and supportive pages, see the  [LINK: (Help directory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:Contents/Directory] . More can be found at the  [LINK: (request directory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Request_directory] .

[LINK: (Click here to ask for help on your talk page) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Special:MyTalk&action=edit&section=new&preload=Help:Contents/helpmepreload&preloadtitle=Help+me%21] . A volunteer will visit you there shortly!

Alternatively, you can ask a question via the #help channel at  [LINK: (Wikipedia:Discord) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:Discord]  or  [LINK: (IRC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IRC]  chat at the #wikipedia-en-help   [LINK: (connect) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wikipedia:IRC_help_disclaimer]  channel.

Wiki Education offers a library of training modules for new Wikipedia editors and students.

